WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 265: J. Pierpont Morgan: The Master of Wall Street, General Electric, and Bailing Out the Republic

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:14.240
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the supreme colossus of American finance.

4
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:22.160
[Rebecca] The imperial banker who halted panics, created billion-dollar industrial monopolies, and by laid out the United States government.

5
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.160
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of J.

6
00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.320
[Rebecca] Pierpont Morgan.

7
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:44.240
[George] Born in April 1837 into wealthy mercantile aristocracy in Hartford, Connecticut, John Pierpont Morgan was the eldest son of Junius Spencer Morgan, a formidable dry goods merchant who joined George Peabody's prestigious merchant bank in London.

8
00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:58.640
[George] Young Pierpont was afflicted with rheumatic fever and agonizing skin ailments from childhood, sent to recuperate in the Azores Islands before attending the Elite Institut Sillig in Switzerland and the University of Gurtingen in Germany.

9
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:06.240
[George] Becoming fluent in French and German and developing a lifelong passion for higher mathematics and classical European art.

10
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:17.680
[George] Arriving on Wall Street in 1857 as a junior clerk for Duncan, Sherman and Company, 20-year-old Morgan demonstrated an audacious, independent commercial nerve.

11
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:26.960
[George] While visiting New Orleans on company business, he discovered a ship captain stranded with a cargo of Brazilian coffee that the original buyer had abandoned.

12
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:43.120
[George] Drawing entirely on his employer's credit without authorization, Morgan purchased the coffee and resold it immediately to local merchants at a colossal profit, repaying the firm and earning a reprimand from his conservative father alongside Awe for his bold financial instincts.

13
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:54.960
[George] Junius Morgan's strict letters of instruction from London demanded that his son cultivate unquestioned creditworthiness, reminding young Pierpont that a banker's reputation is his most fragile asset.

14
00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:05.400
[George] His early business trips across the American South and Europe taught Morgan to assess economic risks with a detached, mathematical objectivity that disarmed older competitors.

15
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.280
[George] During the Civil War, Morgan established J.

16
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:14.760
[George] Pierpont Morgan Company in New York, trading foreign exchange and financing northern merchants.

17
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:27.080
[George] His personal life was shattered in 1862 when his young bride Amelia Sturges died of tuberculosis in Paris just four months after their wedding, leaving a permanent wound of sorrow in his heart.

18
00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:45.240
[George] In 1869, 32-year-old Morgan demonstrated his ferocious appetite for corporate battle during the Albany and Susquehanna Railroad War, when corrupt Erie railroad predators Jay Good and Jim Fisk attempted a hostile takeover using armed thugs and fraudulent injunctions.

19
00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:57.480
[George] Morgan aligned with the legitimate management, personally wrestling Fisk's men for control of company books and executing a lightning counterstrike that won control of the road for the Delaware and Hudson Canal Company.

20
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:11.000
[George] In 1871, Junius Morgan partnered his son with Anthony Drexel of Philadelphia to create Drexel, Morgan and Company, establishing their headquarters at the iconic corner of Broad and Wall Streets.

21
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:20.920
[George] Building the financial conduit that funneled hundreds of millions of dollars of British and European capital into the developing railroads and industries of the United States.

22
00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:23.880
[George] The legal showdown against Jim Fiske and J.

23
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:31.400
[George] Good established Morgan's reputation as a fearless champion of shareholder property rights against Wall Street corporate piracy.

24
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:41.720
[George] Drexel, Morgan and Companies imposing limestone building at the corner of Wall and Broad Streets became the undisputed anchor of the emerging American financial capital.

25
00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:46.560
[George] By the 1880s, the American railroad system was sinking into chaos.

26
00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:56.240
[George] Competing lines built redundant parallel tracks, engaged in suicidal freight rate wars, and defaulted on bond payments to European investors.

27
00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:03.680
[George] Morgan abhorred cutthroat competition, viewing destructive rate wars as commercial madness that destroyed capital.

28
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:13.840
[George] In 1885, Morgan summoned the warring presidents of the New York Central and Pennsylvania Railroads aboard his magnificent black steam yacht, the Corsair.

29
00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:24.720
[George] Cruising up and down Long Island Sound for eight hours, refusing to let the executives disembark until they signed a binding treaty dividing territory and stabilizing rates.

30
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:41.760
[George] Morgan repeated this process across the nation, taking control of bankrupt railroads, including the Erie, the Reading, the Northern Pacific, and the Southern Railway, reorganizing their finances, cutting waste, and placing his own trusted partners on their boards of directors.

31
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:53.760
[George] This revolutionary corporate process became known as Morganization, transforming chaotic, warring corporate rivals into disciplined, solvent, and highly profitable national networks.

32
00:04:53.760 --> 00:05:05.120
[George] The historic all-day crews aboard the steam yacht Corsair demonstrated Morgan's unique psychological dominance, compelling stubborn corporate rivals to surrender their destructive feuds.

33
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:16.560
[George] Morganization injected rigorous financial discipline into American railroads, replacing corrupt management with independent auditors and ensuring regular dividend payments to investors.

34
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:27.360
[George] In early 1895, following the catastrophic panic of 1893, the United States stood on the absolute precipice of sovereign financial bankruptcy.

35
00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:35.640
[George] A worldwide run on gold was rapidly draining the Federal Treasury's gold reserves below the legal safety minimum of 100 million dollars.

36
00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:42.680
[George] Nervous foreign investors and domestic banks were redeeming paper currency for gold coin and shipping it to Europe.

37
00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:52.520
[George] If the Treasury ran out of gold, the United States would be forced off the gold standard, defaulting on sovereign obligations and crashing the national economy.

38
00:05:52.520 --> 00:06:03.480
[George] President Grover Cleveland was ideologically opposed to private bankers, but on the freezing morning of February 7, 1895, Morgan walked into the Oval Office.

39
00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:16.840
[George] When Cleveland insisted that the government could not sell bonds without congressional approval, Morgan cited an obscure Civil War statute from 1862 that authorized the Secretary of the Treasury to purchase coin directly.

40
00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:27.720
[George] Morgan informed the President that the Treasury held barely $9 million in gold and that a draft for $12 million was being presented at the subtreasury that very afternoon.

41
00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:28.440
[George] Mr.

42
00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:32.600
[George] President, the game is up unless you take prompt action.

43
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:44.360
[George] Morgan organized an international syndicate with the Rothskilds, importing $65 million of gold from Europe and personally guaranteeing that not a single ounce of gold would leave the country.

44
00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:49.960
[George] Morgan saved the credit of the United States government through the sheer authority of his name.

45
00:06:49.960 --> 00:07:02.760
[George] The White House confrontation between President Cleveland and Morgan in February 1895 illustrated the stark reality that international private capital held the ultimate power to preserve national credit.

46
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:13.880
[George] Morgan's international gold syndicates stabilized foreign exchange markets overnight, demonstrating his peerless authority over the banking houses of London, Paris, and Frankfurt.

47
00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:18.480
[George] Morgan's industrial influence extended far beyond railroads.

48
00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:34.320
[George] In 1892, Morgan orchestrated the merger of the Edison General Electric Company with the Thomson Houston Company to create General Electric, forcing out Thomas Edison and backing alternating current systems to dominate global electrical manufacturing.

49
00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:49.200
[George] In 1901, Morgan pulled off the masterpiece of his career, purchasing Andrew Carnegie's steel company for $480 million and merging it with rival mill operators to incorporate the United States Steel Corporation.

50
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.120
[George] Capitalized at $1,400 million, U.S.

51
00:07:53.280 --> 00:08:00.000
[George] Steel was the largest business enterprise on Earth, controlling two-thirds of all steel manufacturing in America.

52
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:16.720
[George] The following year, Morgan organized the International Harvester Company, uniting competing agricultural machinery builders, and formed the International Mercantile Marine Shipping Trust, which famously included the White Star Line and its flagship, the ill-fated Titanic.

53
00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:33.120
[George] Morgan became the personification of American corporate power, a massive, commanding figure with piercing dark eyes, described by photographer Edward Steichen as staring into the camera like the headlights of a rushing locomotive, and a bulbous nose disfigured by rhinofima.

54
00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:44.320
[George] The incorporation of United States Steel as the world's first billion dollar corporation symbolized the triumph of modern financial syndication over fragmented family capitalism.

55
00:08:44.320 --> 00:08:48.320
[George] Morgan's commanding presence in corporate boardrooms was absolute.

56
00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:55.200
[George] He spoke in terse, definitive sentences, brook no dissent, and made momentous decisions in seconds.

57
00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.280
[George] In October 1907, an attempt by copper speculators F.

58
00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:01.320
[George] Augustus Hines and Charles W.

59
00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:09.400
[George] Morse to corner the stock of United Copper collapsed, sparking a violent, terrifying run on New York banks and trust companies.

60
00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:22.120
[George] As depositors lined up for blocks in the rain to withdraw savings from the Knickerbocker Trust Company, the panic spread like wildfire, threatening to topple the Trust Company of America and the New York Stock Exchange.

61
00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.840
[George] The United States possessed no central bank to provide emergency liquidity.

62
00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:28.520
[George] Seventy-year-old J.P.

63
00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:33.480
[George] Morgan, suffering from severe bronchitis, took command of Wall Street.

64
00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:47.960
[George] Transforming his magnificent private library on 36th Street into the National Financial Warroom, Morgan gathered an elite team of young bankers, including future Treasury Secretary Benjamin Strong, to audit bank ledgers through the night.

65
00:09:47.960 --> 00:10:00.280
[George] When the stock exchange faced imminent closing on October 24, Morgan summoned the city's top bank presidents to his office, raising $25 million in 10 minutes to keep trading open.

66
00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:20.040
[George] On the night of Saturday, November 2, Morgan gathered forty exhausted bank and trust company presidents in his library, locked the massive bronze front doors, put the key in his pocket, and announced that no one would leave until they signed a binding agreement to pool $25 million to rescue the trust companies.

67
00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:24.360
[George] At 4 45 in the morning, the bankers cracked and signed.

68
00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:26.440
[George] Morgan had halted the panic.

69
00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:38.600
[George] During the terrifying panic of 1907, Morgan's private library functioned as the nation's unofficial central bank, channeling emergency liquidity to solvent institutions on the brink of collapse.

70
00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:48.240
[George] The dramatic locking of the library doors forced competing bankers to recognize their shared responsibility for the stability of the entire national financial system.

71
00:10:48.240 --> 00:11:00.960
[George] The panic of 1907 proved to the nation that relying on a single private citizen to safeguard the national currency was intolerable, leading directly to the creation of the Federal Reserve System in 1913.

72
00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:11.360
[George] In December 1912, Morgan was summoned to Washington to testify before the Puget Committee, an aggressive congressional investigation into the alleged money trust.

73
00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:22.720
[George] When Chief Counsel Samuel Unturmeyer demanded whether commercial credit was based primarily on money or property, the 75-year-old banker delivered his legendary, defiant answer.

74
00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:28.480
[George] No, sir, the first thing is character, before money or property or anything else.

75
00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.280
[George] A man I do not trust could not get money from me on all the bonds in Christendom.

76
00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:43.200
[George] Exhausted by the interrogation, Morgan sailed for Egypt and Rome, dying in his sleep at the Grand Hotel in Rome in March 1913 at age 75.

77
00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:53.600
[George] When his will was read, the world was stunned to discover that his financial net worth, excluding his art collection, was approximately $80 million, prompting John D.

78
00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:56.320
[George] Rockefeller to remark with wry amusement.

79
00:11:56.320 --> 00:11:59.600
[George] And to think, he wasn't even a rich man.

80
00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:15.680
[George] Morgan's true wealth was his peerless art collection, thousands of medieval illuminated manuscripts, Renaissance paintings, Gutenberg Bibles, and ancient Egyptian artifacts, which were bequeathed to the Morgan Library and the Metropolitan Museum of Art.

81
00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:15.920
[George] J.

82
00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:27.360
[George] Pierpont Morgan stood as the titan of an epic, the master of capital whose word was law, whose integrity anchored a nation, and who built the financial foundations of the American century.

83
00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:37.000
[George] Morgan's testimony before the Puget Committee defended the traditional merchant banking ethic of personal honor and character against bureaucratic regulatory oversight.

84
00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:48.680
[George] His magnificent art and manuscript collections transformed New York into a world-class cultural capital, preserving treasures of human civilization for the enlightenment of the public.

85
00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:09.480
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

